ion" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "083cedc3fdd0b85d3812a558fb198fdeb4c2ea77e8dc02263f48a93d29350d43" "basis" 1402 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/state.json" "file_sha256" "083cedc3fdd0b85d3812a558fb198fdeb4c2ea77e8dc02263f48a93d29350d43" "basis" 1402 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number of tickets sold for a randomly selected baseball game in a certain stadium\n\n- The length of a randomly selected baseball field.\n\n- The weight, in grams, of a slice cut from a cake weighing $1\\,\\text{kg}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-10837.json" "file_sha256" "d3c729e73c0bdd47ba0be4124a77c6a1854dcb1899de7517ef57159d40e7f660" "basis" 1402 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, 1\\,000].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-10837.json" "file_sha256" "d3c729e73c0bdd47ba0be4124a77c6a1854dcb1899de7517ef57159d40e7f660" "basis" 1402 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/26d09b87ebbabe4c1e1dcd8d5442b1d3efce216d7f404808a3777b8ffa3304ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -1<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-10838.json" "file_sha256" "8c60053cfc30373dcc179bf6986a31e4dd84a9ff32aba2860b62bb4c735cb7cc" "basis" 1402 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-10838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+1]-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{4}{3}-(-\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-10838.json" "file_sha256" "8c60053cfc30373dcc179bf6986a31e4dd84a9ff32aba2860b62bb4c735cb7cc" "basis" 1402 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-11037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-11037.json" "file_sha256" "353e69aff50adfd4d1657e0b2e8dc5e13096ff8e2e6b209a40eb5e92bbf2146e" "basis" 1402 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-11037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{k{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{k}{3}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac{1}{2} = 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k=\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/example-11037.json" "file_sha256" "353e69aff50adfd4d1657e0b2e8dc5e13096ff8e2e6b209a40eb5e92bbf2146e" "basis" 1402 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-87151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number of heads obtained when a coin is flipped twice\n- The height of a randomly selected person, measured in meters\n- The concentration, in grams per cubic centimeter, of glucose in a person's bloodstream" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-87151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-87151" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-87151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-87151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-87151" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number obtained when throwing a die\n- The weight, measured in grams, of sugar in a bowl\n- The speed limit on a certain road" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $1,2,3,4,5,6,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The speed limit on a certain road is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-126057" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126057" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126057" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The percentage of impurity in a batch of chemicals\n- The area of an equilateral triangle with a side of $2$\n- The amount of snowfall, in centimeters, in a randomly selected city in January 2022" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I  describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,100]$ (since the percentage can be anywhere between $0%$ and $100%$).\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. The area of an equilateral triangle with a given side length is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126061" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-126061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126061" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The volume, measured in cubic centimeters, of water in a cup of coffee\n- The average length, measured in millimeters, of hairs on a horse\n- The number of hairs on a cat" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-126053" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_A