ate.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-87154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nSome common examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $[0,1].$\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are heads or tails, which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-87154" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-87154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-87154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-87154" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/87c5e1b3307b2a42fff6a97408c0475dbe242bef4a9dbfbabcec407b3fa23ce1.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3},\\, & 0\\le x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}(1)^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158425" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-158425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158425" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/ae6d25fae4127a74b38bcb9c0de8f44705dca81353c89557047e5d35a3465c5b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =4-1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158427" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/495b8aaac849e90ffd46f34f35dd4d3783c2c4fa35c3887c339387d31215d7af.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{4},\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{x+1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+2]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+0]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-158414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158414" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{1}^{3}}{2}]-[\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{0}^{3}}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158419" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/8835c085ca34d59f5c79786e12ed67cb33ccfdcfefd706dcc83ca2be0239670f.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{3}]-[{0}^{3}] \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-158315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-86915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 2<x\\le 10, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-86915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{10}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{2}^{10}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{2}^{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{10}+\\frac{k}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{5}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-86915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-86915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-86915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-86915" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k},\\, & 1<x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{k}{\\int }_{1}^{4}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}([\\frac{64}{3}+4]-[\\frac{1}{3}+1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{k}(21+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=24.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "24"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158447" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "24"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{k},\\, & 3\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{x}{k}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{5}\\frac{x}{k}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2k}]}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =(\\frac{25}{2k}-\\frac{9}{2k}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2k} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{k}=1\\,⟹\\,k=8.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158444" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-158444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158444" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k,\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}+kx]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{8}{12}+2k]-[0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}+2k=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158437" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-158437" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158437" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-158437" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}},\\, & 3\\le x\\le 9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{k}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{3}^{9}k{x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{k}{x}]}_{3}^{9} \\\\  & =-\\frac{k}{9}+\\frac{k}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2k}{9}=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "6841ce231457aa7882e09d9b64586cdf7426ad574511cd3e175f9dca27145835"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{9}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "q-98912" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{9}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/state.json" "file_sha256" "71ae0c9661a354767e2c0683cd8d66e332178b17209b9135b7b3d7ba5c70a2db"} {"question" "e-10837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n- The number of tickets sold for a randomly selected baseball game in a certain stadium\n\n- The length of a randomly selected baseball field.\n\n- The weight, in grams, of a slice cut from a cake weighing $1\\,\\text{kg}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/example-10837.json" "file_sha256" "2319a4c197d6c2964714d6ab2bc1e7edd1bdb0afd43311dd87410600a8504b22"} {"question" "e-10837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,\\ldots,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, \\infty).$\n\n- Statement III describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real numbers in the interval $(0, 1\\,000].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/example-10837.json" "file_sha256" "2319a4c197d6c2964714d6ab2bc1e7edd1bdb0afd43311dd87410600a8504b22"} {"question" "e-10838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/26d09b87ebbabe4c1e1dcd8d5442b1d3efce216d7f404808a3777b8ffa3304ff.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/7e6f6662d9597683762bcc48164e5d1227ae6a1a6bdea4243a3a3f4ca314e10a.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^1 f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x = 1$\n\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/example-10838.json" "file_sha256" "84eeb3eac802d9410e4c4783b3709377c5fe11bd60a86441cc311f908bb64847"} {"question" "e-10838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{3}{8}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}+1]-[\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}+(-1)]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}([\\frac{1}{3}+1]-[-\\frac{1}{3}-1]) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}(\\frac{4}{3}-(-\\frac{4}{3})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statements I and II are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/example-10838.json" "file_sha256" "84eeb3eac802d9410e4c4783b3709377c5fe11bd60a86441cc311f908bb64847"} {"question" "e-11037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/assets/2bad707ee54e56c4171b99b13a36135522ab83069ab3b0291f3254850502226d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/example-11037.json" "file_sha256" "3a4a1f7e7a93d4edcd005bbe3af6290e36b2d5f63975af8b4df99b6e27a71772"} {"question" "e-11037" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1} (2x^3+kx^2) \\, \\textrm dx = 1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{3}+k{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{k{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{k}{3}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac k3+\\dfrac{1}{2} = 1 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad k=\\dfrac{3}{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073533/example-11037.json" "file_sha256" "3a4a1f7e7a93d4edcd005bbe3af6290e36b2d5f63975af8b4df99b6e27a71772"}] "usage" {"q-158315" [] "e-10838" [] "q-126053" [] "q-87154" [] "q-158437" [] "e-11037" [] "q-158419" [] "q-98912" [] "q-158427" [] "e-10837" [] "q-158447" []} "basis" 2701 "retractions" [[17592186305032 :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A *continuous* random variable is a random variable whose possible values are represented by an interval of real numbers.\n\nCommon examples of continuous random variables are shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/05e3d893f6c069dce5b51dc7d9fa5830b44bca58fa071009548fde20ebe92c19.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement II describes a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values correspond to the real interval $(0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III does *not* describe a continuous random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, but its possible values are the integers $0,1,2,3,…,$ which do not correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186106458 :question/problem "Which of the following are continuous random variables?\n\n1. The weight, in kilograms, of sugar in a randomly selected bag with a capacity of $1kg$\n2. The number of students in a randomly selected classroom\n3. The outcome of flipping a coin"] [17592186305053 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/ae6d25fae4127a74b38bcb9c0de8f44705dca81353c89557047e5d35a3465c5b.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function"] [17592186305039 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040017/assets/4acac98ef7276cb1e90557ca161666a33ccf0a7627b9ebfcbbe44508b9e5f5bb.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\\, & 0<x\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are t